## 2. Ranking (speedup x how common)

1. Compressed IPC read (zstd 20x, lz4 1.5x): every compressed Arrow stream from pyarrow/Arrow C++ users. Phase 1.
2. filter/take on string/binary/list (60-140x): the default filter path for real tables. Phase 1.
3. concat N-ary (16-29x): chunk flattening, take/filter fallbacks, user concat. Phase 1.
4. validateLayout window (O(length) instead of O(offset+length)): every RecordBatch.init on a slice. Phase 1.
5. Selection indices word path (3x, filter int32 2.4x): every filter. Phase 1 (cheap).
6. Chunked datum filter/take (133 ns/row now; target about 7): Table-level filter. Phase 2-B.
7. Struct take (3092 ns/row; target about 20): filter/take on struct columns. Phase 2-A.
8. N-ary list/struct concat (250 ns/row at 20k parts; target about 5). Phase 2-C.
9. zstd context reuse (about 2x on small-batch writes). Phase 2-D, optional.

## 3. Phase 1 (done: perf2.patch)

Apply: `git apply <scratchpad>\perf2.patch` from the repo root (fb56ff9). 13 files, +1180 / -438.

### T1 bitmap: word-at-a-time bit copy (`src/bitmap.zig`)
Adds `pub fn copyBits(dst, dst_off, src, src_off, len)` and `pub fn setBits(dst, off, len)`. Head bits go
one at a time until `dst` is byte-aligned. Then one destination byte per step: `@memcpy` when the source is also
aligned, else `(src[si+k] >> shift) | (src[si+k+1] << (8-shift))`. Tail bits go one at a time.
Test "copyBits and setBits match a bit-by-bit reference": 500 random offset/length trials.
Done when: test passes on both compilers.

### T2 N-ary flat concat (`src/concat_array_refs.zig`)
- New `concatManyFlat(allocator, dt, parts) Error!?ArrayRef` for null, bool, all fixed widths,
  fixed_size_binary, string/binary (i32), large_string/large_binary (i64). It returns null for other types.
  It sums lengths and null counts once, allocates each output buffer once, and copies each part once:
  fixed width via `@memcpy`, bool via `copyBits`, binary via rebased offsets (`o +% shift`) plus one `@memcpy` of bytes.
  It ends with `validateLayout` + `ArrayRef.fromOwnedInlineBuffers`.
- `concatArrayRefs` calls it first: `if (try concatManyFlat(allocator, data_type, parts)) |out| return out;`.
- `concatTwoArrayRefs` also calls it first, so nested list/struct children concat through the same kernel.
- Deleted (dead after the dispatch): `concatNullArray`, `concatBooleanArray`, `concatFixedWidthArray`,
  `concatVariableBinaryArrayI32/I64`, `isValidAt`, `bitAt`.
- `concatValidityBuffer` (still used by pairwise list/struct/fixed_size_list) and the new `concatValidityMany` use
  `appendValidityBits`. It leaves all-null parts as zero bytes, uses `setBits` for null_count==0 or no bitmap,
  and `copyBits` otherwise.
- Test "concatArrayRefs N-ary path matches per-row values for int32, string and bool parts": 9 uneven parts.
Done when: concat probe numbers as in section 1; all concat tests pass.

### T3 direct gather for string/binary/list (`src/compute/datum_permutation.zig`)
- `takeBinaryArray(comptime T, allocator, src, indices)`. Pass 1 bounds-checks every index and offset range
  (`offsetRange`) and sums bytes. Pass 2 writes offsets, bytes and validity. On a T overflow it returns `error.Overflow`.
- `takeListArray(comptime T, ...)`: rebuilds offsets/validity, expands child indices, then takes the child once by
  recursing into `datumTakeArrayLikeNullable` (so list<string> and list<list<...>> stay fast).
- Dispatch in `datumTakeArrayLikeNullable` (the `if (datum == .array)` block):
  `.string, .binary` -> i32; `.large_string, .large_binary` -> i64; `.list` -> i32; `.large_list` -> i64;
  `else => takeFixedWidthArray`. A null result falls through to the old slice+concat path.
- Test "compute datumTake direct gather for string and list on sliced input with null indices".

### T4 word-at-a-time filter selection (`datumFilterSelectionIndices`, same file)
- `appendBoolSelections` reads 8 predicate rows per step (`byteAtBit` handles any bit offset).
  emit = `values & validity`, plus `| ~validity` when `drop_nulls == false`; emits indices via `@ctz`.
  A first popcount pass sizes the list exactly (no regrowth).
- `.array` predicate: one call. `.chunked`: one call per chunk with `base` = logical start; stops at `logical_len`.
  `.scalar` keeps the old per-row loop.
- Test "compute datumFilterSelectionIndices word path matches per-row decisions on sliced predicate"
  (offset 3, 21 of 23 rows, both drop_nulls modes, plus the out-of-range error).

### T5 validateLayout scans only the window (`src/array/array_data.zig`)
`validateOffsetsI32/I64`, `validateOffsetsSizesI32/I64` and `validateByteViews` take a `start` argument;
call sites pass `self.offset`. Before/after (I32 shown, I64 identical):
```zig
// before
var prev: i32 = offsets[0];
var i: usize = 1;
// after
var prev: i32 = offsets[start];
var i: usize = start + 1;
```
Only `[offset, offset + length]` is reachable through the array, so this matches Arrow semantics.

### T6 compressed IPC read decodes in place (`src/ipc/stream_reader.zig`)
`decodeRecordBatchBody`:
1. Pass 1 reads each buffer's 8-byte uncompressed length (`decodedChunkLen`; -1 = stored raw) and lays out the
   decoded body with 8-byte padding.
2. One `OwnedBuffer.initUninit(cursor)`.
3. Pass 2 decompresses straight into each slot: `decompressZstdInto` = libzstd `ZSTD_decompress`, already linked,
   which must return exactly the slot length. `decompressLz4FrameInto` = the old LZ4F loop writing into the slot.
   Raw buffers are one `@memcpy`. Padding bytes are zeroed.
`std.compress.zstd`, `decompressZstdPayload` and `decompressLz4FramePayload` are gone. Existing compressed
roundtrip tests and fixtures cover it (stream + file reader both call `decodeRecordBatchBody`).

