## 7. Reviewer risks
1. T5 changes validation semantics. Offsets before the slice window are no longer checked. Only
   `[offset, offset+length]` is reachable through the array; fuzz-corpus passes. A reviewer may still prefer to keep
   full checks in `validateFull`.
2. T6 swaps the zstd decoder from `std.compress.zstd` to libzstd. libzstd decodes frames without a content-size field
   because the capacity is given, and it requires the exact declared length. A hostile 8-byte prefix still makes the reader
   allocate that size before decompressing; this was already true for lz4 and is now true for zstd. Consider a cap.
3. T2 computes `null_count` eagerly for every part (popcount when unknown) and validates the output once.
   Extension types now keep their logical `data_type` on the result directly instead of retagging via `fromBorrowed`.
4. T3/P2-A/P2-B allocate a `?usize` (16 B) per child element; fine for O(n), heavy for very wide lists. The
   ponytail upgrade would be `u32` child indices.
5. T4 `byteAtBit` reads at most one byte past the window but never past the buffer (`i + 1 >= bytes.len` guard).
6. T7 removes flatbuffer trace logging (stderr only). On base, `benchmark-ci` output already failed
   `benchmark_ci_check.py` locally; CI on master may be red for the same reason. Worth confirming.
7. The probe and benchmark speedups are single-machine Windows numbers; 0.16 runs showed noise up to 2x on untouched paths.
